🐛 Describe the bug
The issue seems to be related to Meta key
Repro:
  // auto kFunctorchWrappedTensors = c10::DispatchKeySet({
  //   c10::DispatchKey::FuncTorchGradWrapper,
  //   c10::DispatchKey::FuncTorchBatched});

  auto kTensorSubclassLike = c10::DispatchKeySet({
    // c10::DispatchKey::Batched,
    // c10::DispatchKey::Sparse,
    // c10::DispatchKey::SparseCsrCPU,
    // c10::DispatchKey::SparseCsrCUDA,
    c10::DispatchKey::Meta,
    // c10::DispatchKey::Python
    });

  torch::Tensor tensor = torch::ones({1, 2, 3});
  std::cout << tensor.unsafeGetTensorImpl()->key_set() << std::endl;
  std::cout << (tensor.unsafeGetTensorImpl()->key_set() & kTensorSubclassLike).raw_repr() << std::endl;
The consequence of this is that we always go through the slow, out-of-place path. Example of this happening: #79007 (comment)
Interestingly, printing out the key_set() doesn't show that it has the Meta key, but intersecting with a dispatch key set containing only a meta key gives a non-zero repr.
Versions
main
cc @ezyang @gchanan @zou3519

This key was removed for these dispatches for this comment:



      pytorch/c10/core/DispatchKeySet.cpp


        Lines 17 to 21
      in
      ebb7f42







           // Note [NestedTensor Not Included in Backend Keys] 




           // NestedTensor has been explicitly removed from the "backend keyset" due 




           // to incompatibility with some kernels, so we don't want it to be 




           // included in CompositeImplicitAutograd or CompositeExplicitAutograd 




           // kernels. 





Once autograd has been implemented for NestedTensor explore removing this restriction.
