cc @cpuhrsch

🐛 Describe the bug
When writing a structured kernel, if we write the precomputed parameters with upper case like:
- func: native_layer_norm.out(Tensor input, int[] normalized_shape, Tensor? weight, Tensor? bias, float eps, *, Tensor(a!) out, Tensor(b!) mean, Tensor(c!) rstd) -> (Tensor(a!), Tensor(b!), Tensor(c!))
  dispatch:
    CPU: layer_norm_cpu
    CUDA: layer_norm_cuda
  structured: true
  precomputed:
    - M -> int M
    - N -> int N

It breaks the build because it would generate code something like this:
struct TORCH_API structured_native_layer_norm : public at::impl::MetaBase {

                template <bool M = false, bool N = false>
                struct TORCH_API precompute_out {

                    precompute_out<true, N> set_M(int64_t value) {
                        static_assert(M == false, "M already set");
                        precompute_out<true, N> ret;
ret.M = value;
ret.N = this->N;
return ret;
                    }


                    precompute_out<M, true> set_N(int64_t value) {
                        static_assert(N == false, "N already set");
                        precompute_out<M, true> ret;
ret.M = this->M;
ret.N = value;
return ret;
                    }

                    int64_t M;
int64_t N;
            };
    using meta_return_ty = precompute_out <true, true>;
    meta_return_ty meta(const at::Tensor & input, at::IntArrayRef normalized_shape, at::OptionalTensorRef weight, at::OptionalTensorRef bias, double eps);
};

Here, you can see that the template parameter and actual parameter have conflicting types.
It would be nice to have an error checking early on so that user can know what went wrong early on.
